tel 1.0

base fuel
import soundness

info   the documentation tool of the Soundness ecosystem, as a library
detail
    _Fuel_ compiles a project's Markdown documentation, with the code embedded in it checked
    and animated, and its API, into Pyrocosm's semantic model. `fuel-core` is the document
    model and the conversion of Markdown to blocks; another tool embeds it to show a `Doc` as
    Fuel would.

entry .Doc   one Markdown document, compiled: its path, its title, its blocks and its problems
  detail
      A document is parsed and converted by `Doc.parse`, with its Scala fragments checked by a
      `Checker`, and its references linked by a `Linker`.
  entry .parse      parses and converts one document
  entry .filename   the last segment of a path, without a `.md` extension
  entry #path       the document's path, relative to the directory it was found under
  entry #title      the first heading, else the filename
  entry #blocks     the document as blocks
  entry #problems   the fragments which did not do as their preambles said, each a diagnostic

entry .Corpus   every compiled document, in sections
  entry .Section   one `document` directory: its name, its root, its documents and the files it could not read
    entry #name
    entry #root
    entry #docs
    entry #skipped
  entry #sections   the sections, in the order configured
  entry #size       how many documents there are
  entry #section    a section by its name
  entry #doc        a document by its section and path
  entry #problems   every diagnostic of every document

entry .Blocks   a Markdown document as Pyrocosm blocks, node for node
  entry .of       the blocks of a parsed document, its fences dispatched to the embeddings
  entry .title    the first heading's text
  entry .plain    the text of a run of phrasing, unstyled
  entry .inline   a run of Markdown phrasing as inlines

entry .Linker   where a reference in a document leads: the hook by which prose and code link to the pages which document what they name
  entry #link     a reference as written in prose, or a token by its text and type
  entry #learn    the imports a fragment makes, which extend the scope
  entry #ambiguities   the references which named several entities
  entry .none     links nothing
  entry .token    a highlighted token as the model's, linked
  entry .lines    a fragment's lines as the model's, each token linked

entry .Checker   how a Scala fragment is highlighted: tokenized, or in a warm compiler session
  entry #check    a fragment highlighted as far as its mode asks
  entry #enter    a unit compiled into the session, in scope for every later check
  entry #build    a project's own sources compiled and saved
  entry #compiles whether this checker compiles at all
  entry #context  the session's own context, good only on the compiler's thread
  entry .tokenized   the checker which highlights alone

entry .Checking   the one warm session per compile, on the compiler's thread
  entry .exclusive   runs a body on the compiler's thread
  entry .session     a session on a classpath, with options

entry .Compilation   the compile step: every `*.md` beneath a document directory, read, parsed and converted
  entry .Regime   how a corpus's fragments are compiled
    entry #session    as one session per document, each fragment following the ones before it
    entry #fixtures   the directory of `<document>.scala` fixtures, compiled first
    entry #progress   told of each document as it is done, with its problems
    entry #linker     the linker each document's references resolve through, by its path
  entry .name        a section's name: its directory's last segment
  entry .section     one directory of documents, compiled
  entry .document    one document, compiled, or nothing if it cannot be read
  entry .corpus      every configured directory, compiled
  entry .unreadable  a file which could not be read, as a diagnostic

entry .Embedding   how a fenced block of code becomes blocks: the first embedding to answer a fence wins
  entry #embed       the blocks of a fence, by its info string and content, or nothing if it is not this embedding's
  entry .ScalaCode   a Scala fence, highlighted by the checker, with its preamble honoured; one per document
    entry #embed     the blocks of a Scala (or `fuel`) fence
    entry #problems  the fragments which did not do as their preambles said
  entry .JavaCode    a Java fence, highlighted by harlequin alone
    entry .embed     the blocks of a Java fence
  entry .plain       a fence of any other language, shown plain under its own name
  entry .lines       the lines of a text, as unhighlighted tokens
  entry .trimmed     a fence's content without its trailing newline
  entry .standard    the embeddings every document is converted with

entry .Fragments   a document's blocks as TEL, one document per block, which another tool reads
  entry .tel    the blocks written as TEL
  entry .read   the blocks read back from TEL

entry .Notes   the placing of a note on a fragment by its offsets
  entry .of     a note from a range of a text, across lines or as a caret

entry .Preamble   the TEL preamble a ` ```scala fuel ` fence begins with: Amok's schema, walked by hand
  entry #context       how the fragment is wrapped: top, term or type
  entry #scalaContext  the wrapping as harlequin names it
  entry #id            a name other fragments may follow
  entry #follows       the fragments whose text precedes this one when it compiles
  entry #errors        what a diagnostic means: fail, show or ignore
  entry #mode          how far the compiler goes: parse, typecheck or compile
  entry #language      what the fragment is highlighted as
  entry #marks         the highlights, errors, cautions and parameters marked on the fragment
  entry #frames        the frames of an animation
  entry #autoplay      whether the animation plays when shown
  entry #loop          whether it starts again at its end
  entry #restart       whether a fresh session begins at this fence
  entry #features      the language features turned on from this fence
  entry .Errors   what a diagnostic means: `Fail` (the default), `Show` (errors are expected) or `Ignore`
  entry .Mode     how far the compiler goes: `Parse`, `Typecheck` or `Compile`
  entry .Mark     a range of the fragment marked with a style and a caption
    entry #selection  the range, as named
    entry #style      highlight, error, caution or parameter
    entry #caption    the caption, as Markdown phrasing
  entry .Frame    one frame of an animation: its code, its label, its hold, its time, its language and its marks
    entry #code
    entry #label
    entry #hold
    entry #at
    entry #language
    entry #marks
  entry .empty      the preamble of a fence which has none
  entry .split      a fence's content as its preamble and its code, at the `##` line
  entry .parse      a preamble from its text, or the TEL error
  entry .diagnostic a preamble's TEL error as a diagnostic at its place in the file

entry .Selection   a range of a fragment, named by its first and last characters or literally, refined by line and occurrence
  entry #start       the text the range begins with
  entry #end         the text it ends with, or nothing when it is literal
  entry #line        the line to look on
  entry #occurrence  which occurrence, when the range occurs more than once
  entry #findIn      the range's offsets in a text, or nothing
  entry .parse       a selection from its text
  entry .read        a selection from a mark's atoms
